Here is a simple example of how you can create a Rust app that supports the ElevenLabs.io API and reads the contents of the clipboard aloud using TTS:

First, add the following dependencies to your `Cargo.toml` file:
```toml
[dependencies]
reqwest = "0.11.10"
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
tts = "0.1.0"
```
Next, create a new file called `main.rs` and add the following code:
```rust
use reqwest::Client;
use tokio;
use tts::{Tts, Voice};

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    // Set up the ElevenLabs.io API credentials
    let api_key = "YOUR_API_KEY";
    let api_secret = "YOUR_API_SECRET";

    // Create a new TTS client
    let tts = Tts::new(Voice::English);

    // Get the contents of the clipboard
    let clipboard_contents = get_clipboard_contents().await;

    // Use the ElevenLabs.io API to get the audio URL
    let audio_url = get_audio_url(&api_key, &api_secret, &clipboard_contents).await;

    // Download the audio file
    let audio_file = download_audio_file(&audio_url).await;

    // Play the audio file
    tts.play(&audio_file).await;
}

async fn get_clipboard_contents() -> String {
    // This function should return the contents of the clipboard
    // For now, just return a hardcoded string
    "Hello, world!".to_string()
}

async fn get_audio_url(api_key: &str, api_secret: &str, text: &str) -> String {
    // Set up the ElevenLabs.io API request
    let client = Client::new();
    let url = format!("https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/text-to-speech?api_key={}&api_secret={}&text={}", api_key, api_secret, text);

    // Send the request and get the response
    let response = client.get(url).send().await.unwrap();

    // Get the audio URL from the response
    let audio_url = response.json::<AudioUrl>().await.unwrap().url;

    audio_url
}

async fn download_audio_file(url: &str) -> String {
    // Set up the Tokio HTTP client
    let client = reqwest::Client::new();

    // Send the request and get the response
    let response = client.get(url).send().await.unwrap();

    // Get the audio file contents
    let audio_file = response.bytes().await.unwrap();

    // Return the audio file contents as a string
    audio_file.to_string()
}

struct AudioUrl {
    url: String,
}
```
This code uses the `reqwest` crate to send a request to the ElevenLabs.io API, and the `tts` crate to play the audio file. The `get_clipboard_contents` function should be replaced with a function that actually gets the contents of the clipboard.

Note that you'll need to replace `YOUR_API_KEY` and `YOUR_API_SECRET` with your actual ElevenLabs.io API credentials.

Also, this is just a basic example and you may need to add error handling and other features depending on your specific use case.

